WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.530
Fifty bucks and 2000 articles
on automotive history. The next time the college

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:12.490
sponsors electro program on the auto industry
I think we're going to keep him on our shortlist.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:17.270
The title of his presentation today is "Churchill
in His Own Words." Please welcome Richard

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:20.039
Langworth.
Thank you, Richard.

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:24.890
Richard: Is Larry here?
Yes.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:31.890
Richard: I want to be sure of this.
Thank you for this kind introduction I learned

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.809
so much about myself, I'm very impressed,
I hope you are too.

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:44.010
I'd like to thank Dr. Arnn for all of his
hard work in starting this thing off but not

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:51.010
so much for his quotes. He said today ... He's
just asked me for this, that if you keep a

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:59.270
dog you don't have to bark yourself, meaning
Andrew and me. While we're having lunch I

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:06.550
looked this up in a research resource tool
that we're creating which I spoke to about

00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:12.469
yesterday in which we're trying to put all
of the words in the Churchill Cannon into

00:01:12.469 --> 00:01:15.209
easily searchable digital form, and it's not
there.

00:01:15.209 --> 00:01:22.209
I'm not sure where he got it but I aim to
find out, but there is a good dog quote and

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:30.579
maybe you'd like to hear what he really said.
You know this one anyway.

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:37.380
Speaker 3: [Inaudible 00:01:31].
Richard: Sorry?

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:44.380
Speaker 3: [Inaudible 00:01:33].
Richard: The Axminster [inaudible 00:01:43]?

00:01:44.599 --> 00:01:51.599
You tell me later.
Speaker 3: He says my quote is true.

00:01:54.029 --> 00:02:01.029
Richard: Well, Churchill was invited to the
departure party for Herr von Ribbentrop as

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:06.599
he was leaving his ambassadorial post to go
back to Berlin to do something more important,

00:02:06.599 --> 00:02:12.040
which happened to fall on the day Hitler was
invading the Rhineland. Churchill wondered

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:18.500
why the government had bothered to invite
him and he said, "I thought that if they didn't

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:24.590
bark themselves they wanted the Germans to
know they did have a dog who did bark and

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.480
might bite."
I'd like to give you a picture about where

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:35.530
Winston Churchill stands in the public mind
today as we embark on the Churchill Project

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:42.530
at Hillsdale College as he moves from a figure
familiar to many to a figure for the ages.

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:48.980
The digital age tends to skew the broad image
of Churchill, who he was, what he stood for.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:55.480
It is up to us who study, know, and appreciate
him to follow his daughter Mary's wish to

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.810
keep the memory green and the record accurate.
[Inaudible 00:02:59] very shortly because

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:05.450
I was given only 20 minutes and I've only
overstepped my bounds for another 20 minutes.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:11.280
I'll try to be very concise and speak very
briefly about our difficulties and our assets

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:18.280
in educating people about Churchill, and then
try to give you a net assessment of the two.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:23.810
Let's start with the bad news, for all too
many young people today history turns on the

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:30.810
internet where Churchill fades into the blue
distance of the Middle Ages. Thousands Google

00:03:31.299 --> 00:03:38.290
Churchill, half a billion play Star Craft.
It's not always easy to comprehend, many are

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:44.389
distracted from the truth by sensationalist
fantasy and that's a problem because it tends

00:03:44.389 --> 00:03:48.829
to overwhelm what Churchill teaches us about
life and liberty, about the nature of man

00:03:48.829 --> 00:03:54.439
and free institutions.
This is important because perhaps more now

00:03:54.439 --> 00:03:58.769
than since Churchill's times our liberties
are again under assault, not from anything

00:03:58.769 --> 00:04:05.109
like he had to face but from more insidious
forces, some of them internal. For example

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:09.989
Churchill taught and wrote a good deal about
balancing a proper concern for the general

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:15.430
welfare with the power of the state. He has
advice worth contemplating.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:21.849
Dr. Arnn, as he's already told you has just
written a book about this, Churchill's Trial,

00:04:21.849 --> 00:04:28.849
out this year. He hired Doc Jeffrey, and me
to bet it and nitpick it, which we did and

00:04:31.759 --> 00:04:38.759
as a result he had to rewrite the entire book.
It's a far better product now than it was

00:04:39.349 --> 00:04:46.349
when it started, and thank you Doc Jeffrey,
but the thoughtful, philosophic, pragmatic

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:52.660
Churchill was often buried in a bubbling digital
soup in which to capture your attention he's

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:59.660
made to say or do anything from gassing a
rocky tribesman to firebombing Dresden.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:06.740
Putting words in his mouth is a popular pastime.
Quotations maven Nigel Rees says, "If you

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:11.990
don't know the author of a choice remark,
credit it to Churchill, Einstein, Lincoln,

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:17.860
or Martin Luther King. Everyone will be impressed
and they all said so much that nobody will

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:22.660
know the difference."
Churchill's name elicits 20 to 40 million

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:29.660
Google hits depending on where you enter it,
or when you enter it, many are bizarre accusations

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:38.050
which demand categorial denial such as Churchill
wanted to euthanize the feeble-minded. He

00:05:38.069 --> 00:05:43.979
knew in advance about Pearl Harbor but kept
quiet so as to drag the Americans into the

00:05:43.979 --> 00:05:49.940
war. He exacerbated the Bengal Famine in 1943.
He elect Coventry Burn to protect his secret

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:56.699
intelligence, an actor read his speeches,
he issued sailing orders that sank the Lusitania.

00:05:56.699 --> 00:06:01.470
He was bipolar and an alcoholic. He knew but
did nothing about the Holocaust.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:06.819
Here is my current favorite which was sent
to my website, we welcome all points of view,

00:06:06.819 --> 00:06:12.650
"There are plenty more occasions recorded
throughout history where this wicked old [inaudible

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:19.650
00:06:11] allowed his true persona to be revealed."
If this is not enough to depress you, and

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:24.620
I see very few suicides in the room at the
moment let me make it easier for those of

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:31.289
you who are already inclined in this direction,
especially after Mr. Krauthammer's talk and

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:35.199
sample some of the sheer ignorance generated
by a lot of quality electronic [inaudible

00:06:35.199 --> 00:06:39.870
00:06:34] corner.
Much of what's out there is so poorly edited

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:44.900
or bedded that it threatens to overwhelm what
matters, some of it even relegates our man

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:51.900
into legend. A recent survey in the UK reveal
that 1 in 5 school children think Churchill

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.400
was a mythical figure like King Arthur or
James Bond. Indeed I think more of them think

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.870
007 was real than Sir Winston. When his true
persona does emerge [inaudible 00:07:03] we

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:09.300
are often baffled by it because they lack
instruction in Churchillian ways.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:15.430
"How," one web poster asked, "Could this great
man save Erwin Rommel, the commander of the

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:22.129
Afrika Korp?" "We have a very daring and skillful
opponent against us, and may I say across

00:07:22.129 --> 00:07:28.310
the havoc of war a great general." Now you
and I know the reason which is that with Churchill

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:33.680
what you saw was what you got. He never hid
his admiration for fine qualities even among

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:40.680
enemies, but taken out of context, as it often
is, that remark upsets those who know only

00:07:40.979 --> 00:07:46.819
which side Rommel was on. At least I hope
they know which side he was on.

00:07:46.819 --> 00:07:53.819
I'd just written a book Churchill's Lost Years,
about the 1930's investigating his claim that

00:07:54.969 --> 00:08:01.969
World War II was preventable. It took a chapter
to show how those in-charge … those who

00:08:02.099 --> 00:08:07.539
charge, that he once praised Hitler that they
just can't attach him to the context of his

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:14.430
words. I surely hope that he was right about
Hitler from the start. Dead right. That leads

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:20.400
us to another problem, the view of Churchill
as a one-dimensional figure of World War II.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.289
Even some who profess to admire him see him
only in terms of 1940.

00:08:25.289 --> 00:08:30.210
Sir Martin Gilbert asked to summarize Churchill
in one sentence said nothing about blood,

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:37.210
toil, tears, and sweat. Sir Martin said, "He
was a great humanitarian who was himself distressed

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:43.080
that the accidents of history gave him his
greatest power at a time when everything had

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:48.750
to be focused on defending the country from
destruction rather than achieving his goals

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:53.330
of a fair society."
The problem I think is that many people will

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:58.320
simply believe anything. You can probably
find more pure rubbish about Churchill online

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:04.750
now than all the hypocritical books written
about him in the last century.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:11.750
Churchill's virtues like his faults were on
a grand scale but the virtues extended out

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:19.090
way are false. This is our challenge as educated
people to pound home those virtues. Churchill

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:23.630
once told the future King Edward VIII, "If
you have an important point to make don't

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:29.210
try to be subtle or clever, use a pile driver,
hit the plank once, then come back and hit

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:34.620
it again, and then hit it a third time." The
King certainly took that advice as he pounded

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:40.070
on his choice of wife so well that he had
to abdicate.

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:46.360
But now I hope you are all in despair so now
is the time to go from the bad news to the

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:51.440
not-so-bad-news. The Israeli scholar Daniel
Schriften likes to tell about a member of

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:57.200
the Poland Bureau in Bresner’s time during
the great Russo-Chinese rivalries saying,

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:03.020
"Comrades, I have bad news and good news.
First the bad news, the Chinese have landed

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:10.020
on the moon. Now the good news, all of them."
I wish I had something that would be equivalent

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:18.000
to this [inaudible 00:10:13], but I don't
so let's go on to the not so bad news.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:23.630
In the film Stand Up Guys, a character played
by Al Pacino makes an arresting observation,

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:30.390
"You really die twice," he says, "First when
the last breath leaves your body, and second

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:36.790
when the last person who knew you says your
name for the final time." By that standard

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:42.790
we may conclude that Winston Churchill is
immortal, at least for now. 50 years since

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:47.430
his death those who knew him have dwindled
to a handful and yet his name is still all

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:51.250
over the news.
When the last person who knew him says his

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:57.360
name for the last time Churchill will live
on like Washington, Lincoln, the two Roosevelt's,

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:03.140
but even among those immortals it is Churchill
who dominates the news, the media. "Why?"

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:09.280
I asked the historian David Dilks, the biographer
Neville Chamberlain, we know no one's agree

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:15.830
but no one is a better judge. "Churchill,"
David Dilks said, "Brought to public life

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:21.300
valor of the highest order, not the unthinking
resolution of one blind to danger but the

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:27.080
deliberate courage of a man who appreciates
it, apprehends it all too well." To that he

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:34.080
added what is almost as rare in high politics,
“constructive imagination."

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:40.020
Will he remain immortal 50 years from now?
I don't know, probably yes. I don't know what

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:45.200
will happen in the next 50 years. You'll have
to ask Dr. Arnn, he'll still be in office

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:52.200
and I'm sure he'll give you a very good answer,
but in terms of the present in the next generation

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:57.140
Churchill's immortality is something over
which we may feel confident.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:01.920
The next piece of not-so-bad-news is that
while ignorance is pervasive it is not all

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.310
pervasive through the work of Hillsdale College,
the National Churchill Museum, the Churchill

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:13.140
Center, the Churchill War Rooms, the Churchill
Archives Center and others, the truth is being

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:18.640
disseminated. Public figures are cagey now
when they quote Churchill. Sometimes they

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:23.710
qualify their quotes or come to us even to
see if they're true.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:28.630
Senator John Warner of Virginia quoted the
famous but non-verified Churchillism, "Americans

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:35.180
will always do the right thing, after all
of the possibilities are exhausted." He quickly

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:41.350
added that, "Good authority suggests Churchill
didn't say this, but if not he should have."

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:46.360
Fair enough, it's almost too good for him
not to have said it.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:51.080
The columnist George Will mentioned the genuine
red herring, "The greatest argument against

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:56.750
democracy is a five-minute conversation with
the average voter." Have you heard that one?

00:12:56.750 --> 00:13:01.500
It's all over the web. Many admitted that
Churchill probably never said this at all.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:05.360
Correct. He had more respect than that for
the average voter.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:12.010
At the bottom of all the tributes paid to
democracy he said, "It's the little man walking

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:18.000
into the little booth with a little pencil,
making a little cross on a little bit of paper."

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:23.510
We still vote that way in New Hampshire. "No
amount of rhetoric or voluminous discussion,"

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:29.670
Churchill said, "Can possibly diminish the
overwhelming importance of that point." Of

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:36.670
course he assumed the little man was carrying
a valid form of voter ID.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:42.520
Let me demonstrate how it's possible to counteract
innuendo and falsehood with Churchill in his

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:46.890
own words. He was no fan of India's Congress
Party and he said very rude things about Mr.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:53.350
Gandhi. He will repeat it today. I don't have
to repeat them again. Yet in 1935 after the

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:58.140
government of India act had passed over his
opposition he sent this message, "Tell Mr.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:03.240
Gandhi to use the powers that are offered
and make the thing a success. You have got

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:09.170
the things now make a success, and if you
do I will advocate you getting much more."

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:15.020
Mr. Gandhi has gone very high in my esteem
since he stood up for the untouchables. Now

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:19.470
those who quote Churchill on Gandhi never
quote Gandhi on Churchill. Here is what the

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:26.470
Mahatma replied through Mr. [inaudible 00:14:21]
Dr. Arnn discussed earlier. "I have got a

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:31.220
good recollection of Mr. Churchill when he's
in the Colonial Office, and somehow or rather

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:38.080
since then I have held the opinion that I
can always rely on his sympathy and goodwill."

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:42.270
Gandhi remembered that Churchill stood up
for the rights of the South African Indian

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:49.270
Minority, in 1906, 25 years before when he
was Assistant Secretary for the Colonies.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:56.380
“I was put on to this by a fair-minded Indian
who reminded me that Hitler had rather different

00:14:56.380 --> 00:15:02.270
things in mind for India than the old British
Raj.” A reporter wrote this in the Weekly

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:07.250
Standard during a minor media flop last year
about how furious Churchill would be over

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:11.890
a statue of Gandhi being raised near his in
Parliament Square. I said Churchill would

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:16.820
be fine with it. He'd be pleased that India
has become the world's largest parliamentary

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:21.310
democracy.
What do we draw from all these? That Churchill

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:26.360
was, William Manchester wrote, "Always had
second and third thoughts, and they usually

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:32.260
improve as he went along. It was part of his
pattern of response to any political issue,

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:37.700
that while his early reactions were often
emotional and even unworthy of him they were

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:44.700
usually succeeded by reason and generosity."
In the 1930's when he was warning about Nazi

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:50.760
Germany, Churchill joked about the optimist
and the pessimist. The optimist he said was

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:55.830
a man who did not mind what happened so long
as it didn't happen to him. The pessimist

00:15:55.830 --> 00:16:02.830
was a man who lived with the optimist. Now
on preserving Churchill's legacy the question

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:09.570
we must answer is not whether we are optimist
or pessimist but the question of net assessment,

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:14.230
are we making progress or not?
There are at least five reasons to believe

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:21.230
we are making progress. First is Churchill
himself, he's reliable. You can always count

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:27.820
on his high-mindedness. Martin Gilbert struck
me when he said, "Sometimes I might find that

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:32.990
he was adopting views with which I disagreed
but I always knew that there would be nothing

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:39.990
to cause me to think how shocking, how appalling,
and how many great figures can we say that?"

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:47.180
Second, Churchill hangs around, however profound
the thoughts and decisive the acts of great

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:53.910
personages their relevance tends to diminish
with time. David Dilks says, "For Churchill

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:59.660
the process is somewhat postponed for in the
long life he decided much and influenced more."

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:04.659
Third is Hillsdale College's Churchill Project
which unites and offers two unique resources.

00:17:04.659 --> 00:17:11.110
Sir Martin Gilbert's Churchill favorites and
the 10 million word official biography are

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:16.589
coupled to Hillsdale established skills in
scholarly outreach. You heard about this yesterday

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:21.279
so I won't re-describe it, except to say we
welcome your thoughts and input.

00:17:21.279 --> 00:17:26.740
Fourth, you may consider this odd but let's
face it, we're all a bit odd. Churchill was

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:32.440
fun to study. He's a keen judge of people
with a droll sense of humor. He smiles when

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:37.159
he fights. He represents many sides to many
questions.

00:17:37.159 --> 00:17:43.440
The historian Paul Addison says, "Since history
never repeats itself the policies that Churchill

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:48.720
adopted do not provide ready-made solutions
today but his writings and speeches are full

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:55.720
of reflections and philosophy that offer food
for thought, and it's rare to discover in

00:17:55.830 --> 00:18:02.570
the archives the reflections of a politician
on the nature of men." That is something Hillsdale

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:07.470
has considered since its founding.
As Dr. Arnn explains, "We were founded as

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:12.240
every old Liberal Arts College was founded,
to study the nature of men, his place in the

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:18.659
order of creation." This work much more than
any contemporary political question is the

00:18:18.659 --> 00:18:25.090
heart and work of a college and its main substance.
Fifth and the last, there's always something

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:29.659
new to learn. A [inaudible 00:18:27] once
lamented to Martin Gilbert, "Why you've scarcely

00:18:29.659 --> 00:18:36.549
told 10% of the Churchill story?" Martin replied,
"Really, that much?" Here is an example and

00:18:36.549 --> 00:18:41.750
a challenge and that it shows Churchill wasn't
always right. Writing my book on the 1930's

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:47.759
I was repeatedly warned by scholars and I
will pass this warning on now to students,

00:18:47.759 --> 00:18:53.840
don't be an innocent reader of The Gathering
Storm, ominous warning. It's a great read.

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:58.240
It certainly hooked me but it's not always
the absolute authority. Churchill said, "This

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:05.240
is my case, not history, this is my case."
Here is proof which teaches us much about

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:11.889
the persona of Winston Churchill. It's a damning
entry in the Index under Prime Minister Stanley

00:19:11.889 --> 00:19:18.889
Baldwin. "Confesses to putting party before
country." This refers to a 1936 speech in

00:19:20.370 --> 00:19:25.450
which Baldwin said, "Supposing I had gone
for the country and said that Germany was

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:31.549
rearming and that we must rearm, does anybody
think that this specific democracy would rally

00:19:31.549 --> 00:19:35.950
to that cry at that moment? I cannot think
of anything that would have made the loss

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:38.490
of the election, from my point of view more
certain."

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:45.490
The next day Churchill wrote a friend, "I
have never heard such a squalid confession

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:52.129
from a public man as Baldwin offered us yesterday,"
and he never forgot it. In The Gathering Storm

00:19:52.129 --> 00:19:57.080
he wrote that for Baldwin to avow he had not
done his duty in regard to national safety

00:19:57.080 --> 00:20:03.120
because he was afraid of losing the election.
[Inaudible 00:19:59] quote, "Indecency."

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:08.039
For many years there after it was accepted
that Baldwin had put politics before country

00:20:08.039 --> 00:20:15.039
but Churchill had done Baldwin an injustice.
He quoted him selectively, omitting exculpatory

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:22.240
passages, in fact Baldwin statement referred
to 1933-34 when the country was more pacifist,

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:27.820
and Churchill was both that same day admitted
that Baldwin had fought the 1935 election

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:34.230
on rearmament. Thus Churchill or an editorial
assistant, I think it was his son Randolph

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:40.029
who edited his speeches from which this was
derived, "Unfairly edited Baldwin's defense

00:20:40.029 --> 00:20:46.990
of himself," but the more interesting thing
I found in researching this was, "So what?

00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:51.870
Baldwin is still not wholly vindicated."
To appreciate this I had to read the entire

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:57.600
passage, enhance art with parliamentary debates
from Churchill's speech, which also doesn't

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:03.299
appear in The Gathering Storm. He not only
edited the Prime Minister, he edited himself.

00:21:03.299 --> 00:21:08.659
Churchill said that while Baldwin had stressed
rearmament in the 1935 campaign, he also said

00:21:08.659 --> 00:21:15.090
in the same campaign, "There will be no great
armaments and you can't have it both ways.

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:21.379
You can't build an air force equal to Germany's
without great new armaments."

00:21:21.379 --> 00:21:25.539
Baldwin not only admitted that, had the election
occurred earlier he would not have mentioned

00:21:25.539 --> 00:21:30.580
rearmament because he would have lost. He
also gave mixed messages in the actual election

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:36.059
campaign. His rather like Senator Kerry saying
he was for the Iraq war before he was against

00:21:36.059 --> 00:21:43.059
it, or some of the new candidates for president,
three of which I thought were pretty interesting

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:53.460
guys changing their pronouncements over the
last five years in a matter of hours. One

00:21:56.549 --> 00:22:03.549
of them changed his position on immigration,
another on foreign aid, and the third on the

00:22:06.549 --> 00:22:13.549
ethanol subsidy.
Now it doesn't matter what I think or you

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:20.029
think of the ethanol subsidy, I have a 53
Studebaker I can tell you all about the ethanol

00:22:20.029 --> 00:22:27.029
subsidy but the point is that you don't win
elections by reversing your principles. The

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:36.690
difference in state craft between Baldwin
and Churchill is the duty of a leader in a

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:42.960
free society. Is it the duty of a leader in
a free society to take action that though

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:48.999
to his mind right and necessary jeopardizes
his political survival or back away and hope

00:22:48.999 --> 00:22:55.999
the electric will come around a little later?
Churchill's ringing declaration that same

00:22:57.379 --> 00:23:04.379
year stands in some contrast to what Baldwin
said. "I would endure with patience the roar

00:23:04.389 --> 00:23:09.149
of exultation that would go up when I was
proved wrong because it would lift a load

00:23:09.149 --> 00:23:14.360
off my heart. What does it matter who gets
exposed or discomfited? If the country is

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:20.570
safe who cares for individual politicians
in or out of office?" That's Winston Churchill

00:23:20.570 --> 00:23:27.029
uncompromised even with himself.
Here's another example of how you can learn

00:23:27.029 --> 00:23:30.600
new things by accident. A colleague has said,
"Churchill personally seen the Dardanelles

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:37.539
before those twin World War I tragedies, the
Dardanelles and Gallipoli Campaigns. You've

00:23:37.539 --> 00:23:41.190
probably heard of the plan Churchill wanted
to sail asleep through the straits across

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:47.720
the Sea of Marmara, to fear off Constantinople
and cower the Turks into surrender, relieving

00:23:47.720 --> 00:23:51.179
the Russians who were bottled up in the Black
Sea.

00:23:51.179 --> 00:23:56.059
It was his alternative too, as he told the
Prime Minister, "Chewing barbwire in Flanders."

00:23:56.059 --> 00:24:01.379
The short answer was, "Yes, he had seen the
Dardanelles before it happened, before the

00:24:01.379 --> 00:24:06.840
invasion." The long answer sheds fresh light
on his character. I quickly found that Churchill

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:12.230
visited Turkey in 1910 on a two month’s
cruise of Asia Minor, which brought him to

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:19.230
Constantinople ergo he obviously sails through
the Dardanelles, stopping at Smirnoff I discovered

00:24:20.159 --> 00:24:25.840
he sallied forth on a side trip into Turkey
riding rather incongruously for a minister

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:32.049
of the crown in an iron seat welded to the
caricature of a locomotive.

00:24:32.049 --> 00:24:37.789
Now it's impossible to conceive of his colleagues
like Lloyd George or Asquith riding the rails

00:24:37.789 --> 00:24:43.249
on a caricature. Reflecting on his travels
Churchill wrote the Foreign Secretary Sir

00:24:43.249 --> 00:24:49.980
Edward Gray, "The only view I have formed
about this part of the world of ruined civilizations

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:55.990
and harshly jumbled races is this, why can't
England and Germany come together in strong

00:24:55.990 --> 00:25:01.559
action and for general advantage?"
Pursuing the answer to a simple question I

00:25:01.559 --> 00:25:06.230
stumbled on another example of Churchill's
character, his impulse to seek coalitions

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:12.940
and understanding among the great powers.
All the more interesting he said this in 1910,

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:19.350
before the great Anglo-German naval rivalry
that helped lead to World War I, yet somehow

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:25.980
he could visualize the dangers ahead. Despite
many superficial claims that Churchill was

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:31.679
the chief drum beater for World War I his
impulses were quite the opposite. This story

00:25:31.679 --> 00:25:38.289
belongs in our catalog of his many efforts
to preserve the peace.

00:25:38.289 --> 00:25:43.809
In our net assessment of where Churchill stands
today I think we can say with Professor [Inaudible

00:25:43.809 --> 00:25:49.720
00:25:43] we have a problem but not a dilemma.
It's a problem because the amount of sheer

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:55.730
nonsense hoisted on us daily. Why is it not
a dilemma? Because we know what to do about

00:25:55.730 --> 00:26:00.369
it. To paraphrase one of Churchill's famous
expressions, "We have the tools to finish

00:26:00.369 --> 00:26:04.769
the job."
"You think he was a genius," one critic protested.

00:26:04.769 --> 00:26:10.600
Well, perhaps if we accept one famous definition
of genius, the capacity for taking infinite

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:17.090
pains, Churchill strolled as he said to Neville
Chamberlain, "According to his lights, to

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:22.570
the utmost of his capacity and authority,
which were powerful to save the world from

00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:28.759
awful devastating struggle. Once that struggle
was upon him he didn't win it, that was not

00:26:28.759 --> 00:26:35.190
in his power. What we did was not lose it."
He is said to have suffered from manic depression,

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:39.730
another fallacy. "The things he went through
would depress anybody," his daughter used

00:26:39.730 --> 00:26:46.730
to day. My own description of him is optimistic
realist, especially about humanity. We've

00:26:47.399 --> 00:26:51.570
heard this before in this conference, "The
same imperfect being," he wrote, "Presented

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:57.210
by science were increasingly potent and dangerous
toys, yet man spirit has from the earliest

00:26:57.210 --> 00:27:03.539
dawn of history shown itself upon occasion
capable of mounting to a sublime."

00:27:03.539 --> 00:27:09.769
Professor John Marini speaking at Hillsdale
about the films of Frank Capra, said that

00:27:09.769 --> 00:27:15.519
in later life, "Capra understood that Hollywood
would be changing because the culture in society

00:27:15.519 --> 00:27:22.519
had begun the change. The historical and personal
categories of class and race have become political,

00:27:22.919 --> 00:27:28.149
and self-expression and self-indulgence have
replaced those civic virtues that require

00:27:28.149 --> 00:27:35.149
self-restraint."
Churchill wrote long before World War II,

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:41.509
and this is what the Marini put me on to,
“Democratic governments drift along the

00:27:41.509 --> 00:27:47.919
line of least resistance taking short views,
paying their way with sops and dulls and smoothing

00:27:47.919 --> 00:27:54.360
their path with pleasant sounding platitudes.”
One wonders what Capra and Churchill would

00:27:54.360 --> 00:28:00.480
say today. Perhaps it is well we don't know."
"Churchill was indeed a noble spirit," Martin

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:07.480
Gilbert wrote, "Sustained in his long life
by a faith and the capacity of man to live

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:13.350
in peace, to seek prosperity, and to ward
off threats and dangers by his own exertions.

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:19.739
His love of country, his sense of fair play,
his hopes for the human race were matched

00:28:19.739 --> 00:28:25.710
by formidable powers of work and thought,
vision and foresight. His path had often been

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:31.610
dogged by controversy, disappointment and
abuse but these had never deflected him from

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:38.570
his sense of duty and his faith in the British
people, and to that I would add the American

00:28:38.570 --> 00:28:42.399
people.
The day before he retired Sir Winston told

00:28:42.399 --> 00:28:49.399
some of his ministers, "Never be separated
from the Americans." Nowadays he might advise

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:56.710
the Americans never to be separated from their
friends and to be wary of avowed enemies.”

00:28:58.119 --> 00:29:02.970
Free people in every part of the world are
treading no smooth or easy roads, yet it cannot

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:08.210
be worse than others we've traveled in the
past. "It may well be," Churchill said, "That

00:29:08.210 --> 00:29:14.649
the most glorious chapters of our history
are yet to be written. We ought to rejoice

00:29:14.649 --> 00:29:20.869
at the responsibilities with which destiny
has honored us and be proud that we are guardians

00:29:20.869 --> 00:29:27.869
of our country in an age when her life is
at stake." Thank you.

00:29:37.049 --> 00:29:40.210
Speaker 4: Thank you Mr. Langworth. Now we
have some time for a few questions. If you

00:29:40.210 --> 00:29:47.210
have a question please raise your hand and
one of us will bring a mic to you.

00:30:03.519 --> 00:30:05.389
Speaker 5: Richard, among adult readers -
Richard: Where are you?

00:30:05.389 --> 00:30:06.759
Speaker 5: Who are well-educated and curious
-

00:30:06.759 --> 00:30:11.049
Richard: Where are you? I see, I see what
[inaudible 00:30:08]. Sorry.

00:30:11.049 --> 00:30:16.499
Speaker 5: Among adult readers who are well-educated
and curious but had never been exposed to

00:30:16.499 --> 00:30:23.499
the Cannon of Churchill, if they were to read
one book, which should it be? Before you respond

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:29.720
however, if they said they'd read more than
one which would you suggest should be the

00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:31.960
first one?
Richard: Did you mean by him or about him?

00:30:31.960 --> 00:30:35.299
Speaker 5: By him.
Richard: By him, My Early Life every time,

00:30:35.299 --> 00:30:42.299
right Barry? Where's Barry? Barry, he said
it earlier. My Early Life because it captivates

00:30:45.669 --> 00:30:52.669
this tremendous personality building and it's
written in a very self-deprecating way really.

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:58.409
His wonderful descriptions, his encounters
with mathematics he likens to dark dungeons

00:30:58.409 --> 00:31:05.409
with goblins and demons. With science he went
into a strange cargo of science and acoustics

00:31:05.869 --> 00:31:10.869
and further dim [inaudible 00:31:07] lighted
by sulfurous fires were refuted to contain

00:31:10.869 --> 00:31:17.869
a dragon called the differential calculus,
but I have never met any of these monsters

00:31:18.080 --> 00:31:24.239
since. He was never very good at mathematics.
My Early Life first, then second The Gathering

00:31:24.239 --> 00:31:28.249
Storm, and even though I said it, don't be
an innocent reader, it is his case, he admitted

00:31:28.249 --> 00:31:33.320
it was, it's not history. People say Churchill's
history of the Second World War, not a history.

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:40.320
It's his personal reflections.
General Alan Brooke ... Lord Alan Brooke complained

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:47.460
that, "Winston's published all of his directives
and letters to me but he doesn't quote any

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:53.350
of my replies." Later on went on to record
in his diary some pretty tough letters written

00:31:53.350 --> 00:32:00.350
late at night when he was tired and exasperated.
In his defense he would get letters, messages

00:32:02.090 --> 00:32:07.070
from Churchill, "Pray ascertain for me the
soundings in the bay of Rio De Janeiro and

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:14.070
let me have this by tomorrow's post." No clue
what so ... Oh my God, is he thinking of invading

00:32:17.109 --> 00:32:22.299
Brazil? But that was Alan Brooke and you have
to take it all with a grain of salt. Alan

00:32:22.299 --> 00:32:29.299
Brooke was a great soldier, a brilliant man
and was frustrated working for this thunderbolt,

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:35.970
this tornado.
Those two books anyway, I think. If you can't

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:42.590
get ... The Gathering Storm is what started
me. It was 1965, Churchill's funeral was on

00:32:42.590 --> 00:32:48.059
television. I was in my wife Barbara's cellar
in Staten Island, New York. I like the English

00:32:48.059 --> 00:32:52.749
thing, mostly English cars and I saw this
and I said, "Wow this is quite a character.

00:32:52.749 --> 00:32:59.070
I must read about him." I picked up The Gathering
Storm and the rest is history. I was hooked

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:02.850
but for a while I believed every word in it.
It's only about 99% true.

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:09.850
Any other questions. No? I can't see a thing.
Now I can see.

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:24.980
Speaker 6: Towards the beginning of your presentation
you were talking about the digital age and

00:33:25.429 --> 00:33:30.330
its relation to Churchill and you seem to
kind of infer that the digital age seems to

00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:36.429
be kind of bad for him and his legacy, and
I was wondering if the digital age is the

00:33:36.429 --> 00:33:39.950
problem or if it's the integrity of the digital
age?

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:46.950
Richard: No, I think it's an expression of
a problem, without it where would we be? You

00:33:47.259 --> 00:33:54.259
have ... Churchill knew this you have to march
on with progress and take it as it comes.

00:33:54.279 --> 00:33:59.080
It's just that it is totally un-policed and
there are a lot of people that want to police

00:33:59.080 --> 00:34:05.499
it, I'm not among them but we have tools to
fight back, as I said to counteract innuendo

00:34:05.499 --> 00:34:12.110
and falsehood and we do it pretty well. You
can tell that in people quoting Churchill

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:15.720
in the media, they're a lot more careful than
they used to be.

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:21.960
There's still an awful lot about, "If I were
married to you I'd put poison in your coffee.

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:28.960
If I were married to you Lady Astor, I would
drink it." But you see, half the world now

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:34.100
knows that was Lord Birkenhead who was much
better off than [inaudible 00:34:32] Churchill

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:41.100
was. We are making progress. No, I don't feel
it's all a bad thing, it's just un-policed

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:48.280
and that's the problem with free speech.
Speaker 7: Do we have time for ...

00:34:48.950 --> 00:34:52.070
Speaker 4: We have time for just one more
question.

00:34:52.070 --> 00:34:59.070
Speaker 8: Why do you think that The World
Crisis hasn't gotten more exposure in terms

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:06.770
of Churchill's Cannon?
Richard: When I first ... After I read The

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:11.530
Gathering Storm I read all the other World
War II volumes. In My Early Life I wanted

00:35:11.530 --> 00:35:18.270
to read The World Crisis which I think is
his best example of my case as he called it,

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:24.460
because it was written in the same way, it
was his defense. I look for an order, in those

00:35:24.460 --> 00:35:29.520
days you had to do this un-digitally, finally
found it and the ordered the book, The Crisis

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:34.360
by Winston Churchill. It turned out to be
The Crisis … The Civil War novel by the

00:35:34.360 --> 00:35:39.990
American Winston Churchill, who was a contemporary
and was so well-known in the early part of

00:35:39.990 --> 00:35:44.470
the 20th Century that Winston S. Churchill
had to write him and apologize for the confusion

00:35:44.470 --> 00:35:49.760
that had resulted from their names.
Barbara was saying the other day that we had

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:54.570
the pleasure of taking Lady Soames to the
Library of Dartmouth College where Winston

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:59.170
Churchill, the American’s papers where and
there was the letter from her father to the

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:04.470
American novelist. Mr. Winston Churchill presents
his compliments to Mr. Winston Churchill and

00:36:04.470 --> 00:36:11.470
begs to discuss the subject of mutual concern.
The World Crisis had a long and happy print

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:22.880
run. It was still in print in the 1960's.
Griffin republished it after World War II.

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:28.790
I don't know what its press run is. Barry
you might know how many copies you sell of

00:36:28.790 --> 00:36:35.290
it, but it hasn't had the coverage I think
simply because Second World War just eclipsed

00:36:35.290 --> 00:36:42.290
the Great War, and it was fresh in everyone's
memory and it was so horrific in many ways.

00:36:43.850 --> 00:36:49.990
Britons I think tend to put, like to put or
prefer to put the first World War out of their

00:36:49.990 --> 00:36:56.170
memory because this decimated a generation.
I remember Alex DeCroce saying it was much

00:36:56.170 --> 00:37:03.170
more worse than that because sometimes the
losses are much more greater than one intend,

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:10.250
but the second World War made Winston Churchill
a great man as we said earlier and that's

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:15.910
been the focus. I object to that because that
seems to be for some people the only focus.

00:37:15.910 --> 00:37:22.400
You ask the average Churchill admirer and
they say 1940. They don't know about all the

00:37:22.400 --> 00:37:29.400
other things and there's this huge trove of
stuff that we can get into that we find fascinating.

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:37.840
As Dr. Arnn said he spoke about things that
most politicians never even bothered to consider.

00:37:39.520 --> 00:37:46.520
Does that answer your question?
Speaker 8: Yes, thank you.

00:37:55.260 --> 00:37:58.080
Richard: Thank you.
Speaker 1: Thank you very much Richard. If

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:02.680
you are enjoying this program please consider
joining us on campus in October of this year

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:07.850
when we'll be sponsoring another lecture program
on Winston Churchill. Each of the college

00:38:07.850 --> 00:38:11.840
sponsors four-week long lecture programs on
campus under the auspices of the Center for

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:18.840
Constructive Alternatives or CCA. These programs
consider range of topics related to the college’s

00:38:18.980 --> 00:38:22.710
mission and core curriculum, they feature
a series of guest speakers and conclude with

00:38:22.710 --> 00:38:27.730
a round table analysis of the week's lectures
by members of the college faculty, I guess

00:38:27.730 --> 00:38:33.440
you could call it our version of the Fox All-stars.
Confirmed guest speakers for the Churchill's

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:34.090
CCA Pro -

